CVA6ConfigDcacheIdWidth = 3 Signal Flow Tree
============================================

1. CONFIGURATION PHASE (cv32a6_imac_sv32_config_pkg.sv)
   └── localparam CVA6ConfigDcacheIdWidth = 3;
       └── cva6_cfg.'{ DcacheIdWidth: int'(CVA6ConfigDcacheIdWidth) }

2. BUILD/DERIVATION PHASE (build_config_pkg.sv)
   └── CVA6Cfg.DcacheIdWidth = 3
       └── cfg.DcacheIdWidth = CVA6Cfg.DcacheIdWidth = 3

3. MODULE INSTANTIATION PHASE
   └── std_cache_subsystem (core/cache_subsystem/std_cache_subsystem.sv)
       └── wt_cln_dcache_ctrler (core/cache_subsystem/wt_cln_dcache_ctrl.sv)
           └── parameter DcacheIdWidth = CONFIG.DcacheIdWidth = 3

4. AXI TRANSACTION ID USAGE
   └── wt_cln_axi_adapter (core/cache_subsystem/wt_cln_axi_adapter.sv)
       ├── AXI ID generation:
       │   ├── ARID[DcacheIdWidth-1:0] → ARID[2:0] (3-bit read ID)
       │   ├── AWID[DcacheIdWidth-1:0] → AWID[2:0] (3-bit write ID)
       │   └── ID space: 2^3 = 8 unique transaction IDs
       │
       └── Transaction tracking:
           ├── Outstanding read tracking: Up to 8 concurrent reads
           ├── Outstanding write tracking: Up to 8 concurrent writes
           └── Response matching: RID/BID used for transaction completion

5. OUTSTANDING TRANSACTION MANAGEMENT
   └── Transaction ID Allocation:
       ├── Read transactions:
       │   ├── ID pool: 0-7 for read requests
       │   ├── ID allocation on cache miss
       │   ├── ID tracking for response matching
       │   └── ID deallocation on RVALID response
       ├── Write transactions:
       │   ├── ID pool: 0-7 for write requests
       │   ├── ID allocation from write buffer
       │   ├── ID tracking for write completion
       │   └── ID deallocation on BVALID response
       └── ID reuse: IDs recycled after transaction completion

6. CACHE MISS HANDLING
   └── Read Miss Transaction Flow:
       ├── Cache miss detection
       ├── AXI transaction ID allocation (0-7)
       ├── AXI read request with allocated ID
       ├── Outstanding transaction tracking
       ├── AXI read response with matching RID
       └── Transaction completion and ID release

7. WRITE BUFFER INTEGRATION
   └── Write Transaction Management:
       ├── Write buffer entry gets allocated ID
       ├── AXI write transaction uses entry ID
       ├── Write completion tracking via BID
       ├── Write buffer entry retirement
       └── ID available for reuse

8. PERFORMANCE IMPLICATIONS
   └── DcacheIdWidth = 3 (8 IDs) Impact:
       ├── Advantages:
       │   ├── Up to 8 concurrent read transactions
       │   ├── Up to 8 concurrent write transactions  
       │   ├── Good parallelism for memory accesses
       │   └── Adequate for most cache workloads
       ├── Limitations:
       │   ├── ID exhaustion on high miss rates
       │   ├── Transaction stalls when all IDs allocated
       │   └── Memory controller ID capacity dependent
       └── Alternative sizing:
           ├── Smaller (2 bits): 4 IDs, more blocking
           └── Larger (4 bits): 16 IDs, more parallelism

9. MEMORY CONTROLLER INTERFACE
   └── AXI Infrastructure Requirements:
       ├── Memory controller must support 3-bit AXI IDs
       ├── Response routing based on transaction ID
       ├── Interleaved response handling capability
       └── ID-based transaction completion matching

10. RESOURCE UTILIZATION
    └── Hardware Cost (3-bit IDs):
        ├── ID tracking tables: 8 entries for reads + 8 for writes
        ├── Outstanding transaction state: Address, control per ID
        ├── ID allocation logic: 3-bit ID generation/management
        ├── Response matching logic: RID/BID comparison
        └── Moderate hardware overhead for tracking

11. PROTOCOL COMPLIANCE
    └── AXI4 Protocol Considerations:
        ├── Transaction ID must be preserved through interconnect
        ├── Responses must include matching ID
        ├── Out-of-order completion supported
        ├── ID-based hazard detection
        └── Proper ID lifecycle management

12. CACHE CONTROLLER STATE MACHINE
    └── ID-based State Tracking:
        ├── ID allocation state
        ├── Outstanding transaction state per ID
        ├── Response handling state
        ├── ID deallocation state
        └── ID exhaustion handling

KEY RELATIONSHIPS:
- DcacheIdWidth = 3 enables 8 concurrent AXI transactions (2^3 = 8)
- Supports parallel cache miss handling and write buffer operations
- Balances transaction parallelism vs. hardware complexity
- Critical for cache performance under high miss rates
- Requires AXI infrastructure support for 3-bit transaction IDs